 <h1>What CP-SAT and HiGHS actually do in the whole route</h1>
-<p class="lede">The whole route (<code>awx/whole_chain.sh</code>, on <code>bus622-take5</code>) plans every lane's complete path before it routes anything. That plan is built by <b>two solvers with different jobs</b>. CP-SAT settles <i>discrete</i> questions: who crosses whom, and on which layer. HiGHS then solves a <i>continuous</i> question: how far sideways each lane sits at every point along the way. Everything below is interactive. The toys use the same ideas and constraint shapes as the code, at toy scale.</p>
+<p class="lede">The whole route (<code>awx/whole_route.py</code>, run on a real board by the bus step <code>awx/route_bus.py</code>, on <code>bus622-take5</code>) plans every lane's complete path between two ball grid arrays before it routes anything. That plan is built by <b>two solvers with different jobs</b>. CP-SAT settles <i>discrete</i> questions: who crosses whom, and on which layer. HiGHS then solves a <i>continuous</i> question: how far sideways each lane sits at every point along the way. Between them sits the <b>frame</b>, which turns the board into one road per lane, so both solvers can work along a single coordinate. Most sections below have an interactive toy. The toys use the same ideas and constraint shapes as the code, at toy scale.</p>
 
 <h3>The words you will meet</h3>
 <dl class="gl">
-<dt>lane</dt><dd>one net's planned path through the bus, from the source BGA to the destination BGA.</dd>
-<dt>trunk</dt><dd>the long, parallel stretch of the bus between the two BGAs, where lanes run side by side and may cross.</dd>
-<dt>tooth / berth</dt><dd>where a lane escapes at the source (tooth) and arrives at the destination (berth). Each fixes the layer the lane starts and ends on.</dd>
+<dt>lane</dt><dd>one net's planned path through the bus, from the source BGA to the destination BGA. A differential pair is one lane.</dd>
+<dt>tooth / berth</dt><dd>where a lane escapes at the source (tooth) and arrives at the destination (berth): the end of its escape stub, just outside the array. A surface stub fixes the layer the lane starts or ends on. A stub that ends in a via (a <b>via end</b>) leaves the layer to the solve.</dd>
+<dt>trunk</dt><dd>a straight line from the source array's pad box through the destination's. Lanes that arrive on the destination's facing side end on it.</dd>
+<dt>ring</dt><dd>a road round the destination, outside its pads and the parts hugging it. A lane whose berth is on another side of the destination leaves the trunk and rides a ring to it.</dd>
+<dt>cut</dt><dd>(two meanings) the point on the destination's far side where the two rings meet, which decides which way round each lane goes (<a href="#frame">the frame</a>); and a "not this combination" rule sent back to CP-SAT after a later stage found a plan that cannot be drawn (<a href="#sec-loop">the loop</a>).</dd>
+<dt>u</dt><dd>a lane's route coordinate: distance along the trunk from its tooth, then along its ring to its berth. Every crossing and layer change is a position in <code>u</code>.</dd>
 <dt>crossing</dt><dd>a pair of lanes whose order is swapped between source and destination. They must pass through each other, so at that point they sit on different layers.</dd>
-<dt>via</dt><dd>a layer change. Vias are what the discrete plan tries to minimise.</dd>
-<dt>cut</dt><dd>a "not this combination" rule sent back to CP-SAT after the LP found a plan that cannot be drawn.</dd>
+<dt>via</dt><dd>a layer change. Vias are what the discrete plan tries to minimise. Every via is a through via.</dd>
 <dt>convex</dt><dd>a region with no dents, holes or separate pieces: every point between two allowed points is allowed too. An LP's region always is, which is why the LP finds the best answer overall and not just the best nearby (<a href="#sec-lp">section 2</a>).</dd>
 </dl>
