+<h3 id="frame">0c &nbsp; The frame: one road per lane, the destination unrolled (<code>whole_frame.py</code>)</h3>
+<p>The ends are points round two arrays, but both solvers want each lane to be <b>one road with one coordinate</b>. The frame builds those roads from the board alone.</p>
+<p><b>One lane, worked through.</b> Take a lane whose berth is on the destination's <i>far</i> side, facing away from the source. It leaves its tooth and runs along the <b>trunk</b>, the straight line from the source's pad box to the destination's. In front of the destination it reaches the <b>arrival line</b> (<code>H0</code>). There it leaves the trunk onto the <b>north ring</b>, a road round the destination outside its pads, follows it round the corner and down the far side, and drops into its berth (lane D in the figure below). Its route coordinate <code>u</code> is how far it has come: 32 mm along the trunk, then 23.7 mm along the ring, so <code>u</code> runs from 0 to 55.7. A lane whose berth faces the source never leaves the trunk at all. Every crossing and every via the solve places is a position in <code>u</code>.</p>
+<p><b>Which way round</b> is decided by the <b>cut</b>, one point on the destination's outline away from the facing side. Berths on one side of the cut go round the north, the others round the south. Now <b>unroll</b> the destination: cut its outline there and straighten it into a line. The berths stand in one order, north to south. Unroll the source the same way (its outline cut on its own far side) and the teeth stand in one order too. <b>Two lanes cross exactly when those two orders disagree.</b> Everything the solve needs to know about who crosses whom is in those two lists, so moving the cut changes the crossings, and with them the vias. It also changes how far each lane rides round.</p>
