 <h3>Where else these solvers appear (older braid chain and instruments)</h3>
 <ul>
 <li><b>HiGHS MILP</b>: <code>schedule.exact_pages</code> assigns each net to a page (binary <code>x[i,p]</code>, with <code>x[i,p]+x[j,p] &le; 1</code> for inverted pairs, minimising swimmers, then page vias). <code>joint_floor.py</code> and <code>synth_bus.cap_floor</code> put a binary layer at each crossing (<code>y_a+y_b=1</code>) and count layer changes. That is the same puzzle as 1a, and an infeasible model there means an odd parity cycle. <code>braid._prime_highs_threads</code> pins it to one thread for determinism.</li>
-<li><b>CP-SAT</b>: <code>pages_first.py</code> picks each net's destination move, source move and page in one model (a "swimmer" is priced at 100 vias), and <code>synth_bus.cap_sat_feasible</code> / <code>escape_move_floor</code> check feasibility. <code>whole_ends.py</code> uses it on one worker to rank end states exactly. <code>solve_memo.py</code> caches a solve by hashing the model text plus parameters, and replays a hit by fixing every variable to its stored value. That works because the solve is deterministic.</li>
+<li><b>CP-SAT</b>: <code>pages_first.py</code> picks each net's destination move, source move and page in one model (a "swimmer" is priced at 100 vias), and <code>synth_bus.cap_sat_feasible</code> / <code>escape_move_floor</code> check feasibility. <code>whole_ends.py</code> uses it on one worker to rank end states exactly, and to choose the joint ends on an escape fanout. <code>joint_escape.py</code> plans a whole array's escapes, straps and plane drops in one model. <code>solve_memo.py</code> caches a solve by hashing the model text plus parameters, and replays a hit by fixing every variable to its stored value. That works because the solve is deterministic.</li>
 </ul>
 
